1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,191
The embargo is signed far from the stair, by people who have not held the cups. It arrives on paper, with ribbons, and with soldiers who look embarrassed by the ribbons.

2
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:13,617
Nothing luminous may leave the mountain, and nothing edible may enter, until jurisdiction is signed. Vale reads it aloud. He does not sound like a man who expects the lamps to obey.

3
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:10,213
The lamps stay lit. They were never goods. Asha trims the wicks at the usual hour. The law has no box for an hour.

4
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:13,617
The embargo lists oil, grain, instruments, and ore. It does not define a clay cup carried in a hand. Nima keeps filling cups. She does not hurry, and she does not hide.

5
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:11,489
The trade window is shut and barred. The lamp in that window remains. At night the bar is a dark cross in front of a steady flame.

6
00:15:00,000 --> 00:15:10,638
In the camp, someone sells lamp-like objects to people who miss the stair. They are batteries. Everyone knows. The chord goes on underneath the prices.

7
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:14,043
Sera has the children read the guest law aloud, on the morning the embargo is nailed beside it. The two texts do not match. The children can hear that. So can the soldiers.

8
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:11,489
Small hands pass cups through a gap under the white line. Captain Reza sees them and does not write the report he was issued a form for.

9
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:13,617
Every one hundred and eight seconds the tone returns, on both sides of the ban. Imani measures it and finds no change. A law, she notes, is not a layer of rock.

10
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:12,766
Jurisdiction is a word that can be signed. The river is a fact that can be drunk. The decade ends with the paper in force, and the cups still moving.
